WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.060
A lipstick tube sells for $10 yet holds only about 50 cents of wax, pigment, and oils, leaving

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.320
a wide gap between finished price and raw material cost.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.580
This is First Principles Daily, Episode 88. A standard tube of lipstick reaches store

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:22.460
shelves price between $8 and $15. Inside sits a blend whose raw ingredients, waxes, pigments,

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.720
oils, and fragrance add up to roughly 50 cents when valued at commodity rates. That spread

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.820
points to layers of costs that sit far above the physical inputs themselves. The question

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:38.480
is which of those layers could be trimmed by rebuilding the product and its path to market

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:44.300
from the ground up. Mass market lipstick production begins with small batch mixing of a handful

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.500
of commodity ingredients under controlled temperatures. The resulting paste must then pass

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.580
stability, microbial, and heavy metal tests required by regulators in major markets. Those

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.640
tests are repeated for every shade variation because each new pigment combination is treated

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.920
as a distinct formulation. Once approved, the product moves through contract fillers that

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.640
add their own margins before the brand applies its own markup to cover national advertising

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.860
campaigns. Retail chains further inflate the price with slotting fees and promotional

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:21.220
allowances that secure shelf space. Consumers have grown accustomed to this structure because

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:27.300
brand names, packaging, and in-store displays have long-signaled quality and safety. The system

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.260
rewards incremental shade extensions and seasonal launches rather than simplification of the

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:38.400
core recipe. Supply chains remain fragmented, with pigment suppliers, wax refiners, and fragrance

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:44.060
houses each maintaining separate quality systems. Carrying costs accumulate while inventory

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.080
sits through multi-month approval cycles and seasonal planning windows. The end result

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.660
is a finished price that feels inevitable inside the industry, even though the material

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:58.900
content has stayed inexpensive for decades. The conventional path starts with a formulator

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.860
selecting from a short list of approved waxes such as paraffin or microcrystalline types,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.780
a few iron oxide or organic pigments measured in fractions of a gram, and small volumes

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:15.220
of fragrance and antioxidant stabilizers. Each new shade triggers a separate stability

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:20.380
protocol that checks for separation, color drift, and microbial growth over weeks or months.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:26.220
Because regulators treat every pigment ratio as its own product, the documentation burden

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:31.580
scales directly with the number of stock keeping units a brand wants to offer. Contract

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:37.580
manufacturers then charge per kilogram process plus setup fees for each run, so brands order

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:42.180
modest quantities to avoid excess inventory that might expire or go out of fashion.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:48.240
National advertising budgets are sized to support the resulting high unit price, creating

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.040
a self-reinforcing loop where higher prices fund the campaigns that justify the prices.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:59.100
Retailers, facing limited shelf space, demand slotting fees that can reach thousands of dollars

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:04.640
per item per chain, and these fees are passed straight through to the consumer. The entire

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:12.160
chain therefore treats the current price level as the natural baseline rather than a

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.020
if a magic wand could instantly assemble the finished lipstick from its constituent atoms,

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.700
the dominant expense would be the materials themselves. Typical formulations use several

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:28.200
grams of paraffin or microcrystalline wax, priced in bulk at a few dollars per kilogram,

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:34.460
a few tenths of a gram of iron oxide or organic pigments that cost more per kilogram but appear

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:40.600
in tiny quantities, and trace amounts of fragrance oils and antioxidants. Adding those quantities

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:46.240
at current commodity levels produces a rough material cost near $0.50 per tube, dividing

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:52.080
the common retail price of $0.10 by that $0.50 floor yields an idiot index of roughly

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.900
$0.20. The same ratio holds across the $0.8 to $0.15 range, placing the index between

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:04.480
$0.16 and $0.30. Most of that multiplier does not come from the mixing step itself. Regulatory

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.180
testing and documentation for each shade variation can add several dollars when amortized over

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:15.700
modest production runs. Brand advertising and packaging design consume another large share,

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:22.020
often exceeding the material cost several times over. Retail slotting fees and returns allowances

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:28.000
add still more before the product even reaches the customer. These stages compound because each

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.520
one is sized for the existing high price model rather than for volume that would follow a lower

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:40.040
price. The physical mixing and filling process by contrast is already close to its material limit

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:45.620
once the recipe is fixed. To see the arithmetic more clearly, consider a representative tube

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:51.740
containing three grams of wax at roughly $2 per kilogram, which contributes about six tenths

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:58.160
of a cent, plus one tenth of a gram of pigment at perhaps $20 per kilogram, adding another two

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.760
tenths of a cent, and negligible volumes of fragrance and preservative. Even after allowing

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.200
for modest processing losses and energy during melting and pouring, the total material outlay

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:15.040
stays well below $1. The remaining $9 plus dollars therefore reflect everything that happens

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.540
after the ingredients leave the commodity supplier. Testing laboratories bill for each stability

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:26.540
chamber run and each microbial plate, and those bills are spread across only a few thousand units

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:33.080
per shade. Advertising agencies design campaigns whose media buys are calibrated to the $10 price

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:39.700
so the spend per tube stays high. Retail buyers require proof of cell through before reducing

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:44.520
slotting charges, yet cell through depends on the visibility that the slotting fees themselves

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:50.380
are meant to buy. The result is a closed loop in which no single participant sees an incentive

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:56.320
to lower the price first. A redesign would first target the regulatory pathway by generating a

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:01.980
shared safety database for a narrow set of approved pigments and waxes, allowing new shades to

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:07.940
inherit prior test results rather than repeating full suites. That step would require regulators to

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:13.420
accept aggregated historical data and manufacturers to accept tighter pigment specifications.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:19.780
Second, production would shift to larger, continuous mixing lines that serve multiple brands under

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:25.260
a common formulation, cutting the per unit cost of quality systems and reducing the number of

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:31.420
distinct approval filings. Third, distribution would move toward direct channels or standardized

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:37.100
retail fixtures that lower slotting fees, which in turn would let the selling price fall and volume

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:43.200
rise. Each of these moves depends on measurable preconditions, regulators willing to codify a

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:49.400
limited palette, manufacturers willing to pool testing costs, and retailers willing to accept narrower

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:55.400
margins on higher unit sales. The hardest constraint is consumer willing this to accept fewer branded

00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:01.220
shade variants and simpler packaging. If that preference holds, the price floor remains higher than

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:07.400
the material cost alone would suggest. One objection is that safety testing cannot be short cut without

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:13.520
risking consumer harm. A shared database would still require initial full validation of the core set

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.040
of colorance and would add ongoing monitoring for any new supplier batches, so the safety net would

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:25.120
remain intact while the marginal cost per additional shade dropped sharply. Another concern is that

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:30.760
brands would lose differentiation if they shared formulations. The differentiation could move upstream

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:36.560
to packaging graphics or downstream to marketing narratives rather than to proprietary pigment ratios,

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:43.140
preserving brand identity without duplicating laboratory work. A third worry is that retailers would

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:49.180
refuse lower slotting fees. Higher unit volumes at a lower price point could increase total category

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:55.320
revenue, even if the per-unit margin fell, giving retailers a financial reason to experiment with

00:07:55.320 --> 00:08:01.280
simpler fixtures. None of these shifts removes the need for oversight. Each simply relocates the

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:07.600
oversight to a point where its cost is amortized over far more units. One principle is that regulatory

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:13.120
and marketing layers grow around whatever price the market currently tolerates, so lowering the price

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:18.800
requires changing those layers before the product itself. Another is that an industry whose material

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:23.980
inputs are already measured in sense can only move the selling price by attacking the approval,

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:30.200
distribution, and signaling steps that sit on top of those inputs. Tomorrow we move from an

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:35.880
industry still locked at a high idiot index to one that has already driven its index close to one.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:42.780
Semiconductor-grade silicon wafers. The first visible signal in cosmetics would be a company publishing

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:48.600
a single shared safety file for a fixed set of colorants and inviting others to license it.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:54.900
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown. The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

